+E.6.1 Task, data and problem formulation (Q1–Q10)
+
+   Q1. What problem is TSHC trying to solve?
+   30-second answer. TSHC builds a multi-resolution, human-navigable abstraction of an evolving
+   scientific knowledge hypergraph. Instead of 5,798 fine nodes at once, it produces nested super-nodes at
+   three display scales (at most 12, 40 and 120) for four snapshots. The hard part is not compression but
+   compression that keeps meaning, native hyperedge structure and continuity over time.
+   If they push further.
+   • Input: 5,798 nodes, 1,429 hyperedges, 52 papers. Output: one laminar hierarchy per snapshot plus
+
+       an event log.
+   • Three things make it harder than clustering: higher-order relations, evolution, and names and
+
+       numbers that can be trusted.
+   Common trap. Calling it “a clustering method”. The deliverable is a nested, budgeted, labelled,
+   tracked hierarchy and an evaluation that can be trusted.
+   Where: §1.1, §1.2; abstract.
+
+   Q2. What are P1–P6?
+   30-second answer. P1 laminar refinement, P2 hard size budgets, P3 semantic coherence, P4 hyperedge
+   fidelity, P5 temporal stability with trackable identities, P6 faithful and temporally honest labels. P1
+   and P2 are structural constraints. P3 to P6 are quality requirements about meaning, higher-order
+   structure, evolution and interpretation.
+   If they push further.
+   • Guaranteed: P1, P2 and the structure-preservation half of P4.
+   • Empirical: P3, the degree of P5, and P6 (by a proxy).
+   Common trap. Saying “all six are satisfied”. Only P1, P2 and P4’s preservation are guarantees.
+   Where: §1.2; Table 6; Figure 10.
+
+   Q3. Why is this not just ordinary clustering?
+   30-second answer. Ordinary clustering gives one partition. The task needs a nested hierarchy at
+   several resolutions with hard cluster-count budgets, persistence across snapshots, labels, and preserved
+   hyperedges. It also demands controls against circular evaluation and an extrinsic retrieval task.
+   If they push further.
+   • An algorithm can always output a hierarchy. That is different from showing that the data has
+
+       hierarchical community structure.
+   • The task weights evaluation validity (30%) above method design (25%).
+   Where: §1.1, §2.3 (“Displayed versus natural hierarchy”); assessment text, section 7.
+
+   Q4. Why are hypergraphs necessary here?
+   30-second answer. One extracted relation can involve many entities at once: a single evaluated_on
+   relation can connect a method, several datasets and several metrics. A graph edge joins exactly two
+   nodes, so it cannot state that all of them took part in one relation. In this data 80.5% of hyperedges
+   have arity above 2 and the largest has 65 endpoints.
+   If they push further.
+   • Median arity is 3 in every snapshot; the mean rises from 5.57 (2020) to 6.25 (2026).
+   • An ordinary graph is the special case in which every arity is 2.
+   Common trap. Claiming hyperedges are provably irreducible. The report only guarantees that
+   identity, arity and grouping are kept.
+   Where: §2.1, §3.4; Table 3.
+
+                                         63
+Temporal Semantic Hypergraph Coarsening  E Interview Study Companion
