+Q5. Why is sampling or degree-thresholding not enough?
+30-second answer. They make the view smaller, not more meaningful. They can drop important
+low-degree concepts and give no guarantee that what remains is a coherent idea. TSHC aggregates
+instead of discarding: every original node stays represented in exactly one super-node at each level.
+If they push further.
+• “Every node exactly once per level” is checked by hierarchy.validate.
+• The task text itself names sampling and thresholding as the naive solution.
+Where: Assessment text, section 1; §5.7 (validate).
+
+Q6. Why are the display budgets 12, 40 and 120?
+30-second answer. They are display budgets for human navigation, not estimates of a true number
+of communities. 12 lies inside the 10–15 range the task asks for at the coarsest level, and each level is
+roughly a factor of three finer than the one above.
+If they push further.
+• The task calls the budget a hard display constraint, not a soft objective.
+• That these are useful granularities is listed as assumed, not verified.
+Common trap. Saying “the data has 12 communities”.
+Where: §4.1; Table 21.
+
+Q7. Which snapshots are used?
+30-second answer. Four cumulative snapshots: 2020, 2022, 2024 and 2026. Each contains only the
+nodes and hyperedges that pass the strict visibility rule by that year. Sizes in nodes/edges: 1,505/374,
+2,164/526, 4,164/983 and 5,798/1,429.
+If they push further.
