+global optimum.
+Where: Equation 5; §4.2; Figure 12.
+
+Q21. What is the semantic term S, and why divide by 4|V |?
+
+30-second answer. S is the Ward within-cluster sum of squares of unit MiniLM vectors, divided by
+4|Vt|. The squared distance between two unit vectors is at most 4, so the division gives a bounded
+per-node scale. For unit vectors the mean squared deviation from the centroid is 1 − ∥µC∥2 ≤ 1, so in
+fact 0 ≤ S ≤ 0.25.
+If they push further.
+• S is small when clusters hold texts of similar meaning, and it increases with every merge.
+• The embedded text is “type: surface form”, so identical strings in different roles get different vectors.
+
+Common trap. Assuming S and F span the same range. S is capped at 0.25; F ranges over [0, 1].
+Where: §4.1 (node representation), §4.2.
+
+Q22. What is the structural term F ?
+
+30-second answer. F is the average, over native hyperedges, of the normalised entropy of each edge’s
+
+endpoint distribution across clusters. It rewards keeping all endpoints of a relation in one super-node
+
+and treats each hyperedge as a unit, not as  r  pairs.
+                                             2
