+\begin{studybox}[Why a merge is cheap: every statistic is additive]
+After merging $A$ and $B$ into $C$, \code{Merger.run} does four additions and nothing else:
+\code{count[c] = count[a] + count[b]}, \code{sums[c] = sums[a] + sums[b]},
+\code{inc[c] = inc[a] + inc[b]} (edge multiplicities) and
+\code{previous[c] = previous[a] + previous[b]} (history counts). No member is revisited. The new
+cluster's neighbours are the union of the parents' neighbours, and one fresh $\Delta J$ is
+pushed for each of them.
+\end{studybox}
