+\subsubsection{Task, data and problem formulation (Q1--Q10)}
+
+\QA{What problem is TSHC trying to solve?}
+{TSHC builds a multi-resolution, human-navigable abstraction of an evolving scientific knowledge
+\emph{hypergraph}. Instead of 5{,}798 fine nodes at once, it produces nested super-nodes at three
+display scales (at most 12, 40 and 120) for four snapshots. The hard part is not compression but
+compression that keeps meaning, native hyperedge structure and continuity over time.}
+{\item Input: 5{,}798 nodes, 1{,}429 hyperedges, 52 papers. Output: one laminar hierarchy per
+snapshot plus an event log.
+\item Three things make it harder than clustering: higher-order relations, evolution, and names
+and numbers that can be trusted.}
+{Calling it ``a clustering method''. The deliverable is a nested, budgeted, labelled, tracked
+hierarchy \emph{and} an evaluation that can be trusted.}
+{\S1.1, \S1.2; abstract.}
+
+\QA{What are P1--P6?}
+{P1 laminar refinement, P2 hard size budgets, P3 semantic coherence, P4 hyperedge fidelity, P5
+temporal stability with trackable identities, P6 faithful and temporally honest labels. P1 and P2
+are structural constraints. P3 to P6 are quality requirements about meaning, higher-order
+structure, evolution and interpretation.}
+{\item Guaranteed: P1, P2 and the structure-preservation half of P4.
+\item Empirical: P3, the degree of P5, and P6 (by a proxy).}
+{Saying ``all six are satisfied''. Only P1, P2 and P4's preservation are guarantees.}
+{\S1.2; \cref{tab:properties}; \cref{fig:sc-pmap}.}
+
+\QA{Why is this not just ordinary clustering?}
+{Ordinary clustering gives one partition. The task needs a nested hierarchy at several
+resolutions with hard cluster-count budgets, persistence across snapshots, labels, and preserved
+hyperedges. It also demands controls against circular evaluation and an extrinsic retrieval task.}
+{\item An algorithm can always output a hierarchy. That is different from showing that the data
+has hierarchical community structure.
+\item The task weights evaluation validity (30\%) above method design (25\%).}
+{}
+{\S1.1, \S2.3 (``Displayed versus natural hierarchy''); assessment text, section 7.}
+
+\QA{Why are hypergraphs necessary here?}
+{One extracted relation can involve many entities at once: a single \code{evaluated\_on} relation
+can connect a method, several datasets and several metrics. A graph edge joins exactly two nodes,
+so it cannot state that all of them took part in \emph{one} relation. In this data 80.5\% of
+hyperedges have arity above 2 and the largest has 65 endpoints.}
+{\item Median arity is 3 in every snapshot; the mean rises from 5.57 (2020) to 6.25 (2026).
+\item An ordinary graph is the special case in which every arity is 2.}
+{Claiming hyperedges are provably irreducible. The report only guarantees that identity, arity
+and grouping are kept.}
+{\S2.1, \S3.4; \cref{tab:snapshots}.}
+
+\QA{Why is sampling or degree-thresholding not enough?}
+{They make the view smaller, not more meaningful. They can drop important low-degree concepts and
+give no guarantee that what remains is a coherent idea. TSHC aggregates instead of discarding:
+every original node stays represented in exactly one super-node at each level.}
+{\item ``Every node exactly once per level'' is checked by \code{hierarchy.validate}.
+\item The task text itself names sampling and thresholding as the naive solution.}
+{}
+{Assessment text, section 1; \S5.7 (\code{validate}).}
+
+\QA{Why are the display budgets 12, 40 and 120?}
+{They are display budgets for human navigation, not estimates of a true number of communities.
+12 lies inside the 10--15 range the task asks for at the coarsest level, and each level is roughly
+a factor of three finer than the one above.}
+{\item The task calls the budget a hard display constraint, not a soft objective.
+\item That these are \emph{useful} granularities is listed as assumed, not verified.}
+{Saying ``the data has 12 communities''.}
+{\S4.1; \cref{tab:verified}.}
+
+\QA{Which snapshots are used?}
+{Four cumulative snapshots: 2020, 2022, 2024 and 2026. Each contains only the nodes and
+hyperedges that pass the strict visibility rule by that year. Sizes in nodes/edges:
+1{,}505/374, 2{,}164/526, 4{,}164/983 and 5{,}798/1{,}429.}
+{\item The largest jump is 2022$\to$2024, with $+92.4\%$ nodes.
+\item Cumulative snapshots mean nodes never disappear, hence no death events.}
+{}
+{\S3.3; \cref{tab:snapshots}; \tcode{snapshots.py}.}
+
+\QA{What is the difference between \tcode{origin\us year} and \tcode{first\us seen\us year}?}
+{\code{origin\_year} is when the thing was introduced in the world. \code{first\_seen\_year} is
+the earliest corpus paper that mentions it. TSHC uses \code{first\_seen\_year} for visibility,
+because a snapshot at year $t$ may only assert what the corpus had seen by $t$.}
+{\item This distinction is the hook for P6 temporal honesty named in the task.
+\item Surface forms and pretrained language models are not historically versioned (a stated
+limitation).}
+{Using \code{origin\_year} would let a 2020 snapshot contain an entity the corpus first mentions
+in 2024.}
+{\S3.2; \tcode{snapshots.snapshot}.}
+
+\QA{What is the strict visibility rule?}
+{A node is visible at $t$ iff its \code{first\_seen\_year} is at most $t$. A hyperedge is visible
+iff its own year is at most $t$, the asserting article's year is at most $t$ when known, and
+every endpoint is visible. The whole edge is deferred until all three hold.}
+{\item The endpoint condition prevents dangling edges: \code{h\_00351} to \code{h\_00355} have
+year 2021 but endpoints first seen in 2024.
+\item Consequence: strict edge counts (374, 526, 983) are below the task guide's illustrative
+405, 589, 1063, which used the edge year alone.}
+{}
+{\S3.2 (definition); \tcode{test\us snapshot\us defers\us article}.}
+
+\QA{What data-quality problems did the audit find?}
+{170 hyperedges are dated before the first-seen year of at least one endpoint, and 134 are dated
+before the publication year of the article that asserts them. They are deferred, not silently
+accepted. The benchmark has 18 questions, not 17 as the supplied README says, and dates are
+annual, so the ``by February 2026'' cut-off cannot be enforced.}
+{\item Deferred edges per snapshot: 31, 63, 80 and 0.
+\item Inputs are hashed with SHA-256 and never modified.}
+{}
+{\S3.1, \S3.2; \cref{tab:audit}; \tcode{snapshots.anomalies}, \tcode{scripts/audit\us data.py}.}
+
+\subsubsection{Hypergraphs and projection (Q11--Q19)}
+
+\QA{What information does clique projection lose?}
+{It replaces one arity-$r$ hyperedge by all $\binom r2$ pairs. Once pair weights from
+overlapping hyperedges are summed, you cannot tell which hyperedges produced a pair, what their
+arities were, or how the endpoints were grouped. One ternary relation and three separate pairwise
+relations give the same projected graph.}
+{\item Measured: 1{,}429 hyperedges give 72{,}701 pair instances and 70{,}022 unique pairs;
+1{,}510 pairs (2.16\%) receive weight from more than one hyperedge.
+\item Example: the pair (\code{task\_00001}, \code{task\_00003}) is shared by seven hyperedges.}
+{``Only 2.16\% of pairs collide, so the loss is negligible.'' Arity and grouping are lost for
+every projected hyperedge, not only for the colliding pairs.}
+{\S2.1, \S3.5; \cref{fig:projection}, \cref{fig:sc-projection}; \tcode{hypergraph.project}.}
+
+\QA{How is the pairwise baseline weighted?}
+{An arity-$r$ hyperedge becomes $\binom r2$ pairs, each with weight $1/\binom r2$, so every
+original hyperedge contributes total weight one. For an arity-2 edge the normalised entropy is 1
+if cut and 0 otherwise, so $F$ becomes a normalised weighted cut.}
+{\item Same optimiser, budgets, embeddings and $\alpha=0.5$; $\lambda=0$. That makes it a clean
+ablation of native against projected.
+\item It is a baseline, not the primary representation.}
+{}
+{\S4.9; \cref{tab:variants}; \tcode{hypergraph.project}.}
+
+\QA{What is hyperedge arity?}
+{The number of endpoints of a hyperedge. Here it ranges from 2 to 65 with median 3, and 80.5\% of
+hyperedges have arity above 2 in 2026.}
+{\item Arity enters $F$ through the normaliser $\log r_e$ and the coefficient $1/(r_e\log r_e)$.
+\item Collapse keeps \code{original\_arity} at every level, and the multiplicities always sum to it.}
+{}
+{\S2.1, \S3.4; \cref{fig:arity}.}
+
+\QA{How does TSHC measure hyperedge fragmentation, and why divide by $\log r$?}
+{For each hyperedge it takes the fraction of endpoints in each cluster and computes the Shannon
+entropy of that distribution. The maximum, reached when every endpoint is in a different cluster,
+is $\log r$, so dividing by $\log r$ gives a value in $[0,1]$ that is comparable across arities.
+$F$ is the average over hyperedges.}
+{\item $0$: the whole relation is in one cluster. $1$: fully shattered.
+\item Toy arithmetic: arity 4 split $2{+}2$ gives $0.50$, split $3{+}1$ gives $0.41$.
+\item Without the normaliser an arity-65 edge could contribute up to $\log65\approx4.2$, a pair
+at most $\log2\approx0.69$.}
+{Describing $F$ as a cut count. A cut indicator cannot tell $3{+}1$ from $1{+}1{+}1{+}1$.}
+{\S2.4, \S4.2; \tcode{hypergraph.fragmentation} (reference), \tcode{Merger.delta}.}
+
+\QA{What happens to fragmentation as clusters merge?}
+{At the singleton partition every hyperedge is maximally fragmented, so $F=1$. Merging can only
+bring endpoints together, so $F$ decreases or stays the same, and it reaches 0 when everything is
+one cluster. $S$ moves the other way, which is why the trade-off is real.}
+{\item $\Delta F\le0$ for every merge; it is 0 when no hyperedge touches both clusters.
+\item In the primary 2026 hierarchy 88.8\%, 90.5\% and 92.2\% of hyperedges are fully internal at
+L2, L1 and L0.}
+{}
+{\S4.2, \S4.3; \cref{tab:sizes}.}
+
+\QA{What are the three required T4 collapse cases?}
+{One rule covers all three: map each hyperedge to a multiset of super-nodes. If all $n$ endpoints
+fall in one super-node, the edge becomes an internal edge $\{A{:}n\}$ and is kept. If $1<m<n$ fall
+together, it becomes for example $\{A{:}m,B{:}n-m\}$. If the endpoints span three or more
+super-nodes, it stays a higher-order coarse hyperedge such as $\{A{:}3,B{:}1,C{:}1\}$ and is never
+split into pairs.}
+{\item The multiplicities always sum to the original arity, which the code asserts.
+\item Identifier, relation type, provenance and original arity are kept.}
+{}
+{\S4.5; \cref{fig:collapse}, \cref{fig:sc-collapse}; \tcode{hypergraph.collapse}; \tcode{test\us collapse}.}
+
+\QA{Why keep fully internal hyperedges?}
+{An internal edge is evidence for why the members of that super-node belong together, and the
+label packets use such internal edges. Deleting it would discard provenance and make the coarse
+representation unfaithful to the original relation inventory.}
+{\item They are flagged \code{internal=true}, so a consumer can filter them.
+\item At L0, 1{,}318 of 1{,}429 edges are internal; 111 remain between super-nodes, 24 of them
+spanning three or more.}
+{}
+{\S4.5, \S7.8.}
+
+\QA{What exactly is lost during collapse?}
+{A coarse record says how many endpoints landed in each super-node, but not \emph{which} fine
+nodes inside a super-node took part. That can be recovered from the saved member lists and the
+original input. Parallel edges with identical multisets are kept separately, so the coarse graph
+is larger than a minimal quotient.}
+{\item Kept: identity, arity, multiplicity, relation type, provenance.
+\item Not claimed: that hyperedges carry information no pairwise statistic could encode.}
+{}
+{\S4.5 (``What the rule loses'').}
+
+\QA{Does using native hyperedges prove irreducible higher-order expressivity?}
+{No. The guarantee is narrower: edge identity, grouping, arity and multiplicity are retained and
+used by the objective, whereas the chosen clique projection loses them. The empirical finding is
+that native variants beat this projection on the intrinsic metrics on this corpus.}
+{\item Evidence: pairwise has the lowest coherence, the lowest perturbation ARI, the largest
+giant cluster (5{,}157 nodes at L0) and the most unevaluable labels.
+\item A different pairwise encoding: \nest}
+{Presenting an ablation result as a theorem.}
+{\S4.5, \S8.1.}
+
+\subsubsection{The formal objective (Q20--Q33)}
+
+\QA{What objective does TSHC optimise?}
+{At every snapshot and level it greedily minimises $J=\alpha S+(1-\alpha)F+\lambda T$. $S$ is the
+semantic dispersion of MiniLM embeddings, $F$ is native hyperedge fragmentation, and $T$ is the
+variation of information to the previous snapshot's partition at the same level. The primary
+values are $\alpha=0.5$ and $\lambda=0.2$.}
+{\item $T=0$ in 2020 and whenever fewer than two nodes are shared.
+\item The stopping point is the budget, not a sign change of $\Delta J$.}
+{``Minimises'' means greedy local minimisation under a hard count constraint, not a global
+optimum.}
+{\cref{eq:objective}; \S4.2; \cref{fig:sc-objective}.}
+
+\QA{What is the semantic term $S$, and why divide by $4|V|$?}
+{$S$ is the Ward within-cluster sum of squares of unit MiniLM vectors, divided by $4|V_t|$. The
+squared distance between two unit vectors is at most 4, so the division gives a bounded per-node
+scale. For unit vectors the mean squared deviation from the centroid is $1-\|\mu_C\|^2\le1$, so
+in fact $0\le S\le0.25$.}
+{\item $S$ is small when clusters hold texts of similar meaning, and it increases with every merge.
+\item The embedded text is ``type: surface form'', so identical strings in different roles get
+different vectors.}
+{Assuming $S$ and $F$ span the same range. $S$ is capped at $0.25$; $F$ ranges over $[0,1]$.}
+{\S4.1 (node representation), \S4.2.}
+
+\QA{What is the structural term $F$?}
+{$F$ is the average, over native hyperedges, of the normalised entropy of each edge's endpoint
+distribution across clusters. It rewards keeping all endpoints of a relation in one super-node and
+treats each hyperedge as a unit, not as $\binom r2$ pairs.}
+{\item Range $[0,1]$: 1 at singletons, 0 for a single cluster.
+\item This is where P4 enters the optimiser: the multiplicities $m_{e,C}$ are the collapse output.}
+{}
+{\S4.2; \cref{eq:dF}.}
+
+\QA{What is the temporal term $T$?}
+{$T$ is the VI between the current partition and the previous snapshot's partition at the same
+level, restricted to nodes present in both snapshots and divided by $2\log|U_t|$. New nodes
+contribute nothing, because they have no history to be faithful to.}
+{\item It is level-specific: the $40\to12$ stage is regularised towards the previous level-0
+partition.
+\item Only the temporal variant uses it.
+\item Since $\VI\le\log n$ (\S2.5), $T$ is in fact at most $0.5$; the constant only rescales
+$\lambda$.}
+{}
+{\S4.2, \S4.4 (``The cascade''); \tcode{Merger.previous}, \tcode{temporal\us den}.}
+
+\QA{Why choose $\alpha=0.5$?}
+{To give the two normalised terms equal \emph{nominal} weight without fitting anything to the
+benchmark. The report states that this does not equalise realised influence: $S$ is bounded by
+$0.25$ while $F$ ranges over $[0,1]$, so the structural term pulls harder than its coefficient
+suggests.}
+{\item Sensitivity: $\alpha=0.75$ gives higher L0 coherence ($0.8101$ against $0.8029$) and a
+higher last-transition L0 ARI ($0.964$ against $0.760$).
+\item It was not adopted: that would be outcome-driven tuning on a single-snapshot diagnostic.}
+{``$0.5$ means meaning and structure matter equally.''}
+{\S4.2, \S7.7; \cref{tab:sens}.}
+
+\QA{Why choose $\lambda=0.2$?}
+{It makes history a regulariser and not the dominant force, and it was fixed before any results
+were seen. It is a design choice, not a tuned optimum.}
+{\item The dependence is non-monotone: last-transition L0 ARI is $0.391$ ($\lambda=0$), $0.881$
+($0.1$), $0.760$ ($0.2$) and $0.985$ ($0.4$).
+\item The report's reading: the greedy path interacts with the regulariser in ways a single
+$\lambda$ does not fully control.}
+{Claiming that $0.2$ is optimal.}
+{\S4.2, \S7.7, \S8.2.}
+
+\QA{Why is the structure/semantics trade-off real?}
+{$S$ can only grow as clusters merge, because clusters become less homogeneous, and $F$ can only
+shrink, because more endpoints end up together. The two terms pull in opposite directions at every
+merge, so $\alpha$ sets a real exchange rate.}
+{\item Visible in the results: every structure-aware variant is less coherent than semantic-only
+(2026 L0: $0.798$ to $0.808$ against $0.824$).
+\item And semantic-only is the least stable over time (mean ARI $0.467$).}
+{}
+{\S4.2, \S8.1; \cref{tab:headline}.}
+
+\QA{What is the semantic merge cost?}
+{$\Delta S=\frac{1}{4|V_t|}\cdot\frac{n_An_B}{n_A+n_B}\,\|\mu_A-\mu_B\|^2\ge0$: Ward's increase
+in the within-cluster sum of squares, rescaled. It needs only the two sizes and the two vector
+sums, so it costs $O(d)$.}
+{\item The size factor is about the size of the smaller cluster when the sizes are very unequal.
+\item Code: \code{ds = na*nb/(na+nb) * (diff @ diff) / (4*self.n)}.}
+{}
+{\cref{eq:ward}; \S4.3; \tcode{Merger.delta}.}
+
+\QA{What is the fragmentation merge cost?}
+{Only hyperedges that touch both $A$ and $B$ change. For each such edge the change is the
+coefficient $w_e/(W\,r_e\log r_e)$ times $\phi(m_{e,A})+\phi(m_{e,B})-\phi(m_{e,A}+m_{e,B})$, with
+$\phi(x)=x\log x$. The update is local: the code iterates over the incidence map of the smaller
+cluster.}
+{\item The coefficients are computed once per edge (\code{Merger.coeff}).
+\item Incidence maps add under a merge.}
+{}
+{\cref{eq:dF}; \tcode{Merger.delta}, \tcode{Merger.inc}.}
+
+\QA{Why is $\Delta F\le0$?}
+{Because $\phi(x)=x\log x$ satisfies $\phi(a+b)\ge\phi(a)+\phi(b)$, every bracket in the sum is
+non-positive. In words: combining two categories of a distribution cannot increase its entropy.}
+{\item $\Delta F=0$ exactly when no hyperedge touches both clusters.
+\item This is why hub nodes attract merges: many shared edges mean a large reward.}
+{}
+{\S4.3, \S8.1.}
+
+\QA{How is the temporal merge delta computed?}
+{The engine keeps, for each cluster, the counts of its shared nodes per previous cluster. Merging
+$A$ and $B$ changes only their two rows of the contingency table: $\Delta H(P)$ uses the shared
+totals $s_A,s_B$, and $\Delta H(P,P_{t-1})$ uses the counts $c_{A,k},c_{B,k}$. Then
+$\Delta T=(2\Delta H(P,P_{t-1})-\Delta H(P))/(2\log N)$.}
+{\item If all shared nodes of $A$ and $B$ came from one previous cluster, $\Delta T<0$. If they
+have no previous cluster in common, $\Delta T>0$. A cluster of new nodes only gives $\Delta T=0$.
+\item $H(P_{t-1})$ is constant, so it drops out.}
+{}
+{\S4.3; \tcode{Merger.delta} (\tcode{dh}, \tcode{dj}, \tcode{dt}); \cref{fig:sc-toymerge}.}
+
+\QA{Does the method find the global minimum of $J$?}
+{No. It is deterministic greedy agglomeration over a restricted candidate set. Each step takes
+the exact minimum-cost merge among the \emph{current candidates}, but the merge sequence as a
+whole carries no optimality guarantee.}
+{\item ``That the greedy optimum is close to a global one'' is listed under assumed.
+\item What is verified: the incremental $\Delta J$ equals the full objective difference to
+$10^{-12}$, and the heap returns the true current minimum.}
+{``Exact'' describes the merge costs and the heap, not the solution.}
+{\S4.4 (last paragraph); \cref{tab:verified}.}
+
+\QA{Were $\alpha$, $\lambda$ or $k$ tuned, and what does the sensitivity study show?}
+{Nothing was tuned: the primary values were fixed before benchmark results were seen. The
+one-at-a-time study at 2026 is a dependence diagnostic. $\alpha$ and $\lambda$ change the
+memberships substantially, and $k$ hardly matters (ARI at least $0.93$ against the primary).}
+{\item $\alpha$: ARI against the primary at L0 is $0.698$ ($\alpha=0.25$) and $0.617$ ($0.75$).
+\item $\lambda$: $0.376$ ($\lambda=0$), $0.760$ ($0.1$) and $0.749$ ($0.4$) at L0.
+\item Two results flatter alternatives to the primary setting and are reported anyway.}
+{Presenting the sensitivity table as parameter selection.}
+{\S6.4, \S7.7; \cref{tab:sens}.}
+
+\QA{Which P1--P6 properties are guaranteed and which are empirical?}
+{P1 and P2 are guaranteed by construction and tested. P4's structural preservation is guaranteed;
+whether it gives better partitions is empirical. P3, the degree of P5, and P6 are empirical: the
+VI term encourages stability but guarantees no ARI or VI value, and label faithfulness is measured
+only by a proxy.}
+{\item Proof sketch: only merges, so nested; one merge removes one cluster, so the budget is met.
+\item \code{hierarchy.validate} re-checks every hierarchy before it is written.}
+{}
+{\cref{prop:laminar}; \cref{tab:properties}; \cref{fig:sc-pmap}.}
+
+\subsubsection{Construction algorithm and implementation (Q34--Q48)}
+
+\QA{Why use bottom-up agglomerative clustering?}
+{Every operation merges two existing clusters, so later partitions are coarsenings of earlier
+ones: laminarity is free. The cluster count falls by exactly one per merge, so stopping at 120,
+40 and 12 gives the budgets for free.}
+{\item It also gives closed-form, local merge costs for all three terms.
+\item The price: greedy and without backtracking. An early merge is never undone.}
+{}
+{\S2.3, \S4.4.}
+
+\QA{What is the actual hierarchy cascade?}
+{$P_3$ is all singletons. One continuous merge sequence runs $|V_t|\to120\to40\to12$, and the
+partition is recorded when each budget is reached: $P_2$, $P_1$, $P_0$. Nothing is re-clustered
+between levels.}
+{\item Between stages the hyperedges are re-collapsed onto the current clusters, and the history
+switches to the same level of the previous snapshot.
+\item The candidate graph carries over, re-indexed, with no new proposal search.}
+{Describing it as three independent clusterings.}
+{\cref{alg:tshc}; \S4.4 (``The cascade''); \tcode{hierarchy.build}; \cref{fig:sc-cascade}.}
+
+\QA{Which pairs are allowed as merge candidates?}
+{The union of semantic proposals (each node's 10 nearest MiniLM neighbours, symmetrised) and
+structural proposals (every pair of nodes that co-occur in a hyperedge). The semantic-only variant
+uses only the nearest-neighbour proposals. After a merge, the new cluster's neighbours are the
+union of its parents' neighbours.}
+{\item Structural proposals: 72{,}701 pair instances in the full graph.
+\item If candidates run out, a nearest-centroid fallback exists. It was never triggered.}
+{}
+{\S4.4; \tcode{hierarchy.semantic\us links}, \tcode{hierarchy.build}.}
+
+\QA{Are the structural proposal pairs a pairwise approximation of the objective?}
+{No. Proposals only decide \emph{which} pairs are considered. The cost of every candidate is
+computed with the native objective, from the original hyperedge endpoint multiplicities.}
+{\item Candidate generation and cost evaluation are separate in the code: the \code{links} set
+against \code{Merger.delta}.
+\item Honest caveat: results are proposal-dependent (a listed limitation), although $k$ barely
+matters.}
+{Confusing ``pairs used to propose'' with ``pairs used to score''.}
+{\S4.4.}
+
+\QA{Why use $k=10$ nearest semantic neighbours?}
+{It keeps the candidate graph far smaller than all $n(n-1)/2$ pairs while still proposing
+semantically plausible merges. It was fixed in advance. The sensitivity runs at $k=5$ and $k=20$
+give hierarchies very close to $k=10$: ARI at least $0.93$ at L0 and at least $0.99$ at L1 and L2.}
+{\item A stable sort makes tie-breaking identical on every run.
+\item That $k=10$ is the best value: \nest}
+{}
+{\S4.4; \cref{tab:sens}.}
+
+\QA{What is the lazy heap?}
+{All candidate merge costs sit in a min-heap. After a merge the two parent ids are dead. Entries
+that mention a dead id are stale and are skipped when popped. Entries for the new cluster and each
+of its neighbours are pushed with fresh costs.}
+{\item No decrease-key operation is needed.
+\item Ties are broken by cluster ids, which makes the run deterministic.}
+{}
+{\cref{alg:tshc}; \tcode{Merger.push}, \tcode{Merger.run}.}
+
+\QA{Why can the lazy heap still return the exact current best candidate?}
+{$\Delta J(A,B)$ depends only on the sufficient statistics of $A$ and $B$ and on global
+constants. It changes only if $A$ or $B$ takes part in a merge, and then that cluster disappears
+and gets a new id. So every entry whose two ids are alive carries its current cost, and the
+smallest live entry is the true minimum among current candidates.}
+{\item Verified by \tcode{test\us heap\us matches\us exact\us current\us candidate\us minimum}.
+\item The global constants are $|V_t|$, $W$, $N=|U_t|$, $\alpha$ and $\lambda$.}
+{``Exact'' means exact among current candidates, not globally optimal.}
+{\S4.4 (``Why the lazy heap is exact'').}
+
+\QA{What happens if the candidate graph becomes disconnected?}
+{A deterministic fallback merges the two clusters with the nearest centroids. In all executed
+runs (all variants, snapshots, perturbations and sensitivity runs) it was never triggered.}
+{\item A test covers the fallback and checks that edge mass is preserved.
+\item The stage log records the number of fallbacks per level.}
+{}
+{\S4.4 (``Fallback''); \tcode{Merger.run}; \tcode{test\us disconnected\us candidate\us fallback\us and\us edge\us mass}.}
+
+\QA{What is the main computational bottleneck?}
+{The brute-force nearest-neighbour search, $O(n^2d)$ with $d=384$ (about $3.4\times10^7$
+similarities for $n=5{,}798$), followed by heap work $O((M+Q)\log(M+Q))$. Each $\Delta J$ costs
+$O(d)$ plus the shared incident edges and shared previous clusters. The largest build (temporal,
+2026) took 133 s on a laptop CPU.}
+{\item Hungarian matching is $O(K^3)$ with $K\le120$: negligible.
+\item The full workflow takes 3{,}040 s, dominated by 20 perturbation and 7 sensitivity rebuilds,
+NLI over 832 labels, and the nulls.}
+{}
+{\S4.11, \S5.13, \S7.9.}
+
+\QA{Why is there no scalability claim?}
+{The corpus has 5{,}798 nodes, where brute force is acceptable, and the task excludes scaling
+beyond the sample. Dense candidate sets and many updates can approach quadratic behaviour, so the
+report makes no claim beyond this corpus.}
+{\item Approximate nearest neighbours is the last item of the four-week plan, and only if
+profiling on a larger corpus shows a need.}
+{Quoting 133 s as evidence that the method scales.}
+{\S4.11, \S9.2; assessment text, section 8.}
+
+\QA{How reproducible is the implementation?}
+{Model revisions and package versions are pinned, seeds are fixed (null seed 42; perturbation
+seeds 11, 23, 37, 53, 71), sorting is stable with explicit tie-breakers, and label splits are
+hash-based. Two complete fresh-output runs produced identical scientific metrics, memberships,
+label probabilities and predictions.}
+{\item The embedding cache is keyed by SHA-256 of model, revision and text. A separate-cache
+regeneration of all 18{,}008 embeddings reproduced the cached ones.
+\item Source files are hashed at the start and the end; the run is rejected if any changed.}
+{Reproducibility shows repeatability, not scientific validity.}
+{\S5.5, \S5.13; Appendix~\ref{app:repro}.}
+
+\QA{What do the tests actually protect against?}
+{The 24 tests fall into four groups: optimiser correctness (delta equals the full objective, heap
+minimum, fallback, laminar reproduction), data semantics (collapse multiplicities, edge deferral),
+temporal bookkeeping (birth and death, merge and split thresholds, VI edge cases) and evaluation
+validity (label split, NLI nulls, all-leaf beam equals flat, null reproducibility, sparse
+coherence, partial targets, positive controls, benchmark mutation, source isolation).}
+{\item They target scientific invariants, not line coverage.
+\item \code{verify\_submission.py} re-checks the delivered outputs structurally.}
+{}
+{\S5.13; \cref{tab:sc-codemap}; \tcode{tests/}.}
+
+\QA{Why was spectral hypergraph clustering rejected as the main method?}
+{It does not naturally give nested, hard-budgeted levels, and the temporal term would have to be
+relaxed. TSHC keeps the principle of Zhou et al.\ (penalise cutting a hyperedge, with the edge as
+the unit) but uses entropy fragmentation, which has an exact and cheap merge update.}
+{\item An empirical comparison with a spectral method: \nest\ None was run.}
+{Saying spectral methods are ``worse''. The argument is about fit to the constraints, not measured
+quality.}
+{\S2.4, \S4.12.}
+
+\QA{Why reject a two-stage ``semantic first, structural repair later'' approach?}
+{Two-stage schemes hide the trade-off inside two separate procedures. A single objective with an
+explicit $\alpha$ keeps the exchange between meaning and structure visible and measurable.}
+{\item The task allows a principled two-stage scheme if it is argued, so this is a design choice,
+not a requirement.
+\item That a two-stage scheme would score worse: \nest}
+{}
+{\S4.12; assessment text, task T2.}
+
+\QA{Why reject a learned or neural clustering method?}
+{It would add training assumptions and tuning without any supervision signal, and the task does
+not ask for it (model fine-tuning is out of scope). A small explicit objective is easier to verify
+and to defend.}
+{\item The only learned components are frozen, pinned encoders: MiniLM, BGE and DeBERTa.}
+{}
+{\S4.12; assessment text, section 8.}
+
+\subsubsection{Variants and temporal treatment (Q49--Q57)}
+
+\QA{What are the four compared variants?}
+{\emph{semantic}: $\alpha=1$, $\lambda=0$, nearest-neighbour proposals and Ward cost only.
+\emph{pairwise}: $\alpha=0.5$, $\lambda=0$, clique projection with weights $1/\binom r2$.
+\emph{static}: $\alpha=0.5$, $\lambda=0$, native hyperedges. \emph{temporal}: $\alpha=0.5$,
+$\lambda=0.2$, native hyperedges plus VI to the previous snapshot. Temporal is the primary variant.}
+{\item Same optimiser, budgets and embeddings; only the objective and the candidate set differ.
+\item Each comparison isolates one factor: structure, native against projected, history.}
+{}
+{\cref{tab:variants}; \tcode{hierarchy.build}.}
+
+\QA{Why are static and temporal identical in 2020?}
+{There is no previous snapshot in 2020, so $T$ is set to 0 and $\lambda$ has nothing to act on.
+The two objectives are the same for the first snapshot.}
+{\item In code: \code{lam = cfg["lambda"] if variant == "temporal" and previous else 0.0}.}
+{}
+{\S4.2, \S4.9.}
+
+\QA{What is the difference between temporal regularisation and identity matching?}
+{Regularisation changes the clustering: the $\lambda T$ term makes merges that agree with the
+previous grouping cheaper. Matching happens \emph{after} construction and only decides which new
+super-node inherits which persistent id. Matching is bookkeeping and cannot make an unstable
+clustering stable.}
+{\item Evidence: the static variant also gets matched ids, yet its mean ARI is $0.725$ against
+$0.892$ for temporal.
+\item ``Stabilising by matching only'' is one of the rejected alternatives.}
+{``Hungarian matching stabilises the clusters.''}
+{\S4.6, \S4.12; \cref{fig:sc-temporal}.}
+
+\QA{How does Hungarian matching work here, and why Jaccard?}
+{At each level, compute the Jaccard similarity $|A\cap B|/|A\cup B|$ between every old and every
+new cluster, and solve the one-to-one assignment that maximises total similarity. A new cluster
+matched with non-zero overlap inherits the old persistent id; otherwise it gets a fresh id.
+Jaccard is based on membership, rewards overlap and accounts for size differences.}
+{\item Implementation: \code{scipy.optimize.linear\_sum\_assignment} on the negated matrix, $O(K^3)$
+with $K\le120$.
+\item Fresh ids look like \code{L\{level\}\_\{year\}\_\{index\}}; leaves keep \code{leaf\_<node id>}.
+\item A comparison with other similarity measures: \nest}
+{}
+{\S2.7, \S4.6; \tcode{temporal.track}.}
+
+\QA{What are birth, continuation, growth, merge, split and death?}
+{Birth: a new cluster overlaps no old cluster. Continuation: it inherits an id and acquired no new
+members. Growth: it inherits an id and acquired at least one member. Merge: a new cluster has
+strong overlaps with two or more old clusters. Split: an old cluster has strong overlaps with two
+or more new clusters. Death: an old cluster overlaps no new cluster.}
+{\item Deaths are 0 everywhere, because snapshots are cumulative.
+\item Temporal variant, 2024$\to$2026: 7 births, 84 continuations, 77 growths, 9 merges, 8 splits.}
+{Events describe membership changes, not scientific history.}
+{\cref{tab:events}, \cref{tab:event-counts}; \cref{fig:sc-events}.}
+
+\QA{What counts as a ``strong'' overlap for merge and split events?}
+{At least 2 shared nodes and at least 10\% of the relevant cluster's shared membership: the new
+cluster for a merge, the old cluster for a split. This ignores one-node contamination.}
+{\item Configuration: \code{event\_contribution: 0.1}.
+\item Test: \tcode{test\us split\us merge\us ignore\us one\us node\us contamination}.}
+{}
+{\cref{tab:events}; \tcode{temporal.track}.}
+
+\QA{Are temporal events mutually exclusive?}
+{No. Identity inheritance comes from the one-to-one Hungarian match, while merge and split come
+from the full many-to-many overlap table. A cluster can both inherit an id (growth) and be the
+target of a merge.}
+{\item That is why the event counts of a transition can exceed 172, the number of super-nodes per
+snapshot ($12+40+120$).}
+{}
+{\S4.6.}
+
+\QA{What is lineage retention?}
+{The fraction of nodes shared by two snapshots whose persistent super-node id is unchanged. It is
+a node-level continuity measure that complements the partition-level ARI and VI.}
+{\item Temporal: $0.969$ to $0.985$ in the first two transitions, $0.893$ to $0.897$ in the last.
+\item Static: $0.754$ to $0.901$.
+\item Unlike ARI and VI, it depends on the id assignment made by the matching.}
+{}
+{\cref{tab:vi-lineage}; \tcode{temporal.transition}.}
+
+\QA{Why report both ARI and VI?}
+{ARI counts node pairs and is dominated by very large clusters. VI is information-based and
+responds differently to changes in small clusters. In 2024$\to$2026 they disagree: static has the
+higher ARI at all three levels (L2: $0.853$ against $0.772$), while temporal has the lower
+normalised VI ($0.021$ against $0.045$) and the higher lineage retention.}
+{\item So ``temporal is more stable'' holds on average and by VI and lineage, not at every
+transition by every metric.
+\item The nine ARI values are not independent replications; their mean is descriptive.}
+{``Temporal wins every stability metric.''}
+{\S2.5, \S7.3, \S8.2.}
+
+\subsubsection{Labels and evaluation validity (Q58--Q72)}
+
+\QA{How are label generation and label evaluation separated?}
+{Each cluster is split about 70/30, separately for each node type. The 70\% generation set
+produces the label and the gloss. The 30\% held-out set is the only evidence used for the
+faithfulness check. The code asserts that the two sets are disjoint and together cover the cluster.}
+{\item A type with a single member goes to generation.
+\item A label is evaluable only with at least two held-out non-author members.}
+{The held-out members still come from the same cluster. The split removes text reuse; it does not
+make the check independent of the clustering.}
+{\S4.7; \tcode{labels.split\us members}; \cref{fig:sc-labels}.}
+
+\QA{Why use a hash-based split instead of random sampling?}
+{Ordering the members by SHA-256 of ``seed:id'' is deterministic across runs and needs no mutable
+random state. Stratifying by node type keeps both sets representative of the cluster's
+composition.}
+{\item Seed 42; fraction $0.7$ (\code{label\_generation\_fraction}).}
+{}
+{\S4.7; \tcode{labels.split\us members}.}
+
+\QA{How is the label phrase chosen?}
+{A TF-IDF model over 1--3-grams (English stop words removed) is fitted on the generation members
+of all clusters at that snapshot and level, with authors excluded. The label is the $n$-gram with
+the highest mean TF-IDF weight over the cluster's own generation members, ignoring $n$-grams
+without letters or made only of stop words, and truncated to 6 words.}
+{\item Ties go to the phrase that occurs in the most central representative, then alphabetically.
+\item The fallback label is ``Scientific evidence''.}
+{It often picks generic unigrams: ``materials'', ``feature'', ``layer''.}
+{\S4.7; \tcode{labels.generate}; \cref{tab:l0labels}.}
+
+\QA{How are representative examples chosen?}
+{Non-author generation members are ranked by cosine similarity to the generation-only MiniLM
+centroid. The top two names are quoted in the gloss if they have at most 100 characters.}
+{\item The centroid uses generation members only, so held-out members cannot influence the label.
+\item If neither name is short enough, no name is quoted (the ``---'' rows of \cref{tab:l0labels}).}
+{}
+{\S4.7; \tcode{labels.generate}.}
+
+\QA{What does the gloss claim?}
+{The template is ``This cluster groups evidence concerning $\langle$label$\rangle$, including
+$\langle$rep$_1\rangle$; $\langle$rep$_2\rangle$.'' It claims only that the cluster \emph{contains}
+evidence about the phrase, which is the weakest claim that is still informative.}
+{\item No LLM is used; generation is deterministic.
+\item The price: conservative glosses can be too generic to navigate by.}
+{}
+{\S4.7.}
+
+\QA{What is the overclaim proxy?}
+{The held-out member text (``type: surface form'' lines, capped at 1{,}800 characters) is the NLI
+premise, and the gloss is the hypothesis. An evaluable label scores 1 if the DeBERTa prediction is
+not entailment (neutral or contradiction) and 0 otherwise. The overclaim rate is the mean over
+evaluable labels.}
+{\item Temporal: $0.318$ at L0, $0.308$ at L1, and $54/174=0.310$ overall.
+\item Unevaluable labels stay \code{null}; they are not counted as successes.
+\item Truncation at 512 tokens cuts the premise only, never the gloss.}
+{}
+{\S4.7, \S5.5; \cref{tab:labels}; \tcode{labels.evaluate\us labels}.}
+
+\QA{Why is the NLI score only a proxy?}
+{The DeBERTa model is general-domain, not trained on materials science, and it was never checked
+against human judgement. Vague glosses are easy to entail, so a low rate can reward vagueness, and
+truncation can hide evidence.}
+{\item A high value can also come from generation-only names that are absent from the held-out
+text (\code{notes/statistical\_interpretation.md}).
+\item Replacing the proxy with blinded expert audits is the first item of the four-week plan.}
+{Calling $0.31$ ``the label error rate''.}
+{\S2.9, \S7.5, \S9.1.}
+
+\QA{How is temporal honesty enforced for labels?}
+{Label generation only receives nodes and hyperedges that are visible in the snapshot, and the
+code asserts this before generating. A 2020 label therefore cannot use evidence first seen in 2024.}
+{\item Limit: dates are annual, so material after February 2026 cannot be excluded.
+\item Limit: pretrained encoders and surface forms are not historically versioned.}
+{}
+{\S3.2, \S4.7; assertions in \tcode{labels.generate}.}
+
+\QA{What is the main evaluation circularity hazard?}
+{If MiniLM both builds the clusters and measures their coherence, the method is graded with its
+own ruler and a high score is guaranteed by construction. The same leak applies to labels if the
+faithfulness check uses the text the labeller already saw.}
+{\item A third leak: tuning retrieval on the benchmark that scores it.
+\item The task says this part carries the most weight.}
+{}
+{\S1.2, \S6.1; assessment text, task T6; \cref{fig:sc-barrier}.}
+
+\QA{How is coherence circularity neutralised?}
+{Construction uses MiniLM. Coherence is measured with BGE, a separately trained model family, and
+the code asserts that the two model names differ. Every value is then compared with 100 null
+partitions.}
+{\item Semantic-only stays the most coherent under BGE as well, so that gap is a real property,
+not self-grading.
+\item Assumed, not verified: that BGE similarity reflects an expert's notion of ``same idea''.}
+{``Independent'' means a different model family, not a ground-truth judge.}
+{\S6.1, \S8.3; \tcode{evaluation.execute\us evaluation} (gate H).}
+
+\QA{What does the coherence null model preserve?}
+{Cluster labels are permuted within each node type. Every cluster keeps its size and its exact
+type composition, and only which nodes of a given type sit where is randomised. The code asserts
+both properties.}
+{\item 100 replicates with a fixed seed (42); singletons are treated identically in the
+observation and in the null.
+\item It is a conditional test: conditional on the observed sizes and type counts.}
+{}
+{\S5.11; \tcode{evaluation.coherence}.}
+
+\QA{Why is a type-preserving null stricter than random labels?}
+{Node types already carry semantic signal: a cluster of datasets only looks coherent under any
+encoder. A fully random permutation would create mixed-type clusters and make the method look
+artificially strong. The type-preserving null asks whether the clustering adds coherence
+\emph{beyond} size and type composition.}
+{\item Consequence: small absolute effects ($0.003$ to $0.011$ for temporal) and a very narrow
+null (SD about $0.00015$).
+\item The task's other suggested null (an arity-preserving hypergraph shuffle) was not run: \nest}
+{}
+{\S5.11, \S8.3.}
+
+\QA{Why are all empirical $p$-values $0.0099$?}
+{With 100 null replicates and no replicate reaching the observed coherence, the formula
+$(1+\text{exceedances})/(R+1)$ gives its minimum, $1/101\approx0.0099$. It is a resolution floor,
+not a measured strength of evidence.}
+{\item More replicates would lower the floor; they would not change the effect size.
+\item Twelve comparisons without multiple-comparison correction: descriptive.}
+{``$p=0.0099$ means a huge effect.''}
+{\S2.11; \cref{tab:coherence}.}
+
+\QA{How is perturbation stability evaluated?}
+{At 2026, for each variant, 10\% of the hyperedges (143) are deleted with each of five seeds. The
+hierarchy is rebuilt with the unperturbed 2024 hierarchy as history, and the ARI to the
+unperturbed 2026 hierarchy is computed at levels 0 to 2. Mean, SD and Student-$t$ 95\% intervals
+($n=5$) are reported.}
+{\item Means at L0, L1, L2: temporal $0.781$, $0.818$, $0.869$; static $0.780$, $0.753$, $0.765$;
+pairwise $0.679$, $0.637$, $0.565$.
+\item It measures sensitivity to \emph{current} evidence, not propagated historical damage.
+\item The temporal intervals are wide (SD about $0.1$) and overlap the static ones, so no
+robustness advantage of temporal over static can be claimed.}
+{}
+{\S6.2, \S7.4; \cref{tab:perturb}.}
+
+\QA{Why is the semantic-only perturbation ARI of 1 not impressive?}
+{That variant ignores hyperedges entirely, so deleting hyperedges cannot change its input and the
+hierarchy is identical. The value measures blindness to edges, not robustness.}
+{\item The same variant is the least stable over real time (mean ARI $0.467$).}
+{Reading the top row of the perturbation table as the best result.}
+{\S7.1, \S7.4.}
+
+\subsubsection{Retrieval and benchmark methodology (Q73--Q88)}
+
+\QA{How is benchmark leakage prevented?}
+{Retrieval predictions are written and their SHA-256 is recorded (Gate J) before
+\code{benchmark.py} is imported and the ground truth is read. After scoring, the hash is
+recomputed and asserted equal. Tests check that the construction modules contain no reference to
+the benchmark and that mutating the benchmark does not change the predictions.}
+{\item Annotations for non-exact targets are evaluation-only and are loaded after the freeze.
+\item Honest limit: the benchmark was inspected repeatedly during development, so it is not a
+held-out test set.}
+{A frozen hash prevents leakage inside a run. It does not make the benchmark unseen.}
+{\S5.11 (``Freeze, then score''); \cref{fig:pipeline}; \tcode{test\us benchmark\us isolation\us source}.}
+
+\QA{What are EXACT, ALIAS, PARTIAL, COMPOSITE and ABSENT targets?}
+{EXACT: a node's normalised surface form equals the target. ALIAS: inspected evidence shows that
+another node is the same entity. PARTIAL: only part of the target's identity is present.
+COMPOSITE: the target is a bundle of components. ABSENT: no defensible mapping in the graph. Over
+the 63 occurrences the counts are 48, 1, 11, 1 and 2.}
+{\item Normalisation: NFKC, casefolding, unified typographic hyphens.
+\item Only 28 targets match as \code{method}-type nodes, so any node type is accepted for exact
+identity.
+\item PARTIAL never counts as recovered but stays in the denominator.}
+{}
+{\S5.12, \S6.3; \tcode{benchmark.support\us audit}.}
+
+\QA{What is the difference between direct identity and contextual evidence?}
+{For EXACT and ALIAS targets the target node itself must appear in the top-$k$ ranking. A claim or
+problem document whose context mentions the method does not count. The rule deliberately separates
+``found evidence about X'' from ``returned X as the answer''.}
+{\item A COMPOSITE target counts if all its components appear among the returned documents or
+their context.
+\item This strictness is one of the diagnosed reasons for the $0/63$ result.}
+{}
+{\S5.12, \S8.4; \tcode{benchmark.evaluate\us predictions}; \cref{fig:sc-whyfail}.}
+
+\QA{Why are most expected claims marked unevaluable?}
+{Automatic alignment is strict: only exact claim text plus a resolved matching source article is
+accepted. Semantic nearest neighbours are saved as proposals, not accepted. The graph phrases
+claims differently from the benchmark, so only 1 of 224 expected claims (188 Type A, 36 Type B)
+could be aligned.}
+{\item The others are marked \code{NOT\_EVALUABLE\_ALIGNMENT} instead of being guessed.
+\item The one alignment (Q18.C16) was annotated post hoc, so it is not independent validation.}
+{}
+{\S6.3; \tcode{benchmark.align\us claims}.}
+
+\QA{What does source recovery measure?}
+{The fraction of expected source articles that appear among the source articles of the returned
+documents. It is a coarse topical signal: at $k=10$, flat and beam 3 both reach 75\% on the four
+Type-B questions, and beam 3 reaches 83\% at $k=25$.}
+{\item It does not show that the required claim was retrieved or is entailed.}
+{Presenting 75--83\% as retrieval success.}
+{\S6.3, \S7.6.}
+
+\QA{How is a retrieval document constructed?}
+{Every non-author node becomes a document: its own ``[type] surface form'' line plus at most 12
+deduplicated direct neighbours through content relations, claims first. \code{authored\_by} and
+the broad \code{cites} are excluded. There is no recursive traversal, alias expansion, diffusion
+or re-ranking.}
+{\item The same documents and the same BGE cosine scorer serve both the flat and the beam search.
+\item \code{context\_cap: 12} in the configuration.}
+{}
+{\S4.8; \tcode{retrieval.documents}.}
+
+\QA{What does the flat baseline do, and why are there 5{,}480 documents?}
+{The 2026 snapshot has 5{,}798 nodes, of which 318 are authors; excluding them leaves 5{,}480
+documents. The flat baseline embeds the question with BGE, takes the cosine with every document
+and sorts, so its work is exactly 5{,}480 comparisons per question.}
+{\item A beam wide enough to keep everything reproduces the flat ranking exactly
+(\tcode{test\us all\us leaf\us beam\us equals\us flat\us and\us work\us trace}).}
+{}
+{\S4.8; \tcode{retrieval.search} with \tcode{beam=None}.}
+
+\QA{How does hierarchical beam retrieval work?}
+{Score all level-0 cluster centroids and keep the $b$ best. Score only their children at level 1
+and keep $b$, repeat at level 2, then score every leaf inside the surviving level-2 clusters and
+sort. Documents and scorer are the same as for flat.}
+{\item Work is cluster comparisons plus leaf comparisons.
+\item With $b=3$ the search still scores about 1{,}660 leaves on average, many of them in the
+giant L0 super-node.}
+{}
+{\cref{alg:beam}; \tcode{retrieval.search}; \cref{fig:sc-beam}.}
+
+\QA{Why use normalised cluster centroids?}
+{A cluster is represented by the mean of its members' document vectors, normalised to unit
+length, so its score against the question is a cosine that is comparable across clusters. The risk
+is that a relevant leaf sits in a cluster whose average direction does not look relevant, and is
+pruned.}
+{\item The beam width trades recall against work.
+\item Other cluster representations: \nest\ None was tried.}
+{}
+{\S2.10, \S4.8.}
+
+\QA{What does ``work'' mean in retrieval?}
+{The number of cosine comparisons, cluster plus leaf. It is a deterministic cost proxy. The report
+does not claim that a 69\% reduction in comparisons is a 69\% reduction in latency.}
+{\item Mean work per question: flat 5{,}480; beam 1: 476.9; beam 3: 1{,}711.7; beam 5: 2{,}508.2;
+beam 10: 3{,}030.9.}
+{``The hierarchy makes retrieval three times faster.''}
+{\S4.8, \S9.1; \cref{tab:retrieval}.}
+
+\QA{Why is beam 3 the primary setting?}
+{It was fixed in advance as a moderate setting between work and coverage. Beams 1, 5 and 10 are
+diagnostics. The benchmark result was not used to choose a different beam afterwards.}
+{\item With zero recall for every system, no beam could have been chosen on recall anyway.}
+{}
+{\S4.8; \code{retrieval\_beam: 3} in the configuration.}
+
+\QA{What was the top-10 and top-25 retrieval result?}
+{Every system recovered 0 of 63 Type-A target occurrences at $k=10$ and at $k=25$. Flat used
+5{,}480 comparisons per question and beam 3 used 1{,}711.7, a reduction of 68.8\%. Because flat
+recall is zero, the hierarchy cannot be said to preserve useful recall.}
+{\item Conditional recall over the 61 supported targets is also 0.
+\item Type B: the single aligned claim was recovered in 0 of 10 system and cut-off evaluations.}
+{Leading with ``69\% less work'' and leaving out the zero recall.}
+{\cref{tab:retrieval}; \S7.6.}
+
+\QA{How do you know the evaluator is not simply broken?}
+{A positive control forced each of the 49 EXACT or ALIAS target instances into rank 1 of an
+otherwise unchanged ranking, and the evaluator recovered 49 of 49. Rank inspection shows that the
+gold nodes exist at finite but poor ranks: MatterSim 31, DeepH-E3 57, xDeepH 106, MACE 5{,}437 of
+5{,}480.}
+{\item So the zero is a ranking failure, not a scoring bug.
+\item Test: \tcode{test\us identity\us recovery\us positive\us control\us and\us context\us exclusion}.}
+{}
+{\S7.6 (``Is the evaluator broken?'').}
+
+\QA{Why does flat retrieval fail so badly?}
+{The questions are long descriptions of a problem (``best suited for \dots\ when \dots''). Problem,
+task and claim documents paraphrase such text and win the cosine ranking, while a method document
+is dominated by a short name such as ``MACE''. The representation finds the topical region but
+does not rank the answer entity.}
+{\item Q3: the top three are problem and task nodes whose context mentions DeepH-E3; DeepH-E3
+itself is at rank 57.
+\item Q1: M3GNet 880, CHGNet 1{,}340, NequIP 1{,}536, MACE 5{,}437.}
+{}
+{\S7.6, \S8.4; \cref{fig:sc-whyfail}.}
+
+\QA{Why not fix retrieval after seeing zero recall?}
+{Obvious rescues existed: restrict candidates to method nodes, expand aliases, re-rank. Applying
+them after seeing the result would tune the method on the benchmark that evaluates it. The
+negative result is kept, and the fixes are proposed as pre-registered experiments on a held-out
+question set.}
+{\item The task says honest negative results are valued and unsupported claims cost points.}
+{}
+{\S8.4, \S9.2.}
+
+\QA{What does the retrieval experiment establish?}
+{That the hierarchy reduces comparison work by 45\% to 91\% depending on the beam, and that this
+retrieval design does not rank gold method identities in the top 25, with or without the
+hierarchy. It does not establish downstream utility, because there is no flat recall to preserve.}
+{\item Diagnosed cause: the document and scoring design, plus the dominant cluster for the beam.
+\item That a better document design would make the hierarchy helpful: \nest}
+{}
+{\S7.6 (negative-result box), \S8.4.}
+
+\subsubsection{Results, interpretation and defence (Q89--Q100)}
+
+\QA{What is the key temporal-versus-static result?}
+{Mean cross-snapshot ARI rises from $0.725$ (static) to $0.892$ (temporal), while mean 2026
+coherence across levels falls only from $0.8134$ to $0.8114$. Normalised VI is lower for temporal
+in every transition and level, and lineage retention reaches 97--98\% in the first two transitions.}
+{\item Caveat: in 2024$\to$2026 static has the higher ARI at all three levels.
+\item The mean is over nine dependent values (three transitions, three levels): descriptive.}
+{``Temporal wins everywhere.''}
+{\cref{tab:temporal-ari}, \cref{tab:vi-lineage}; \S8.2; \cref{fig:sc-dashboard}.}
+
+\QA{How should the coherence improvements over the null be interpreted?}
+{They are clear within the 100-replicate test and small in absolute terms. For the temporal
+variant in 2026 the effects are $0.0033$, $0.0062$ and $0.0113$ cosine at L0, L1 and L2.
+Standardised effects of 22 to 70 look large only because the conditional null is very narrow (SD
+about $0.00015$).}
+{\item Once type composition is fixed, much of the apparent coherence is the domain similarity of
+all the texts.
+\item For the temporal variant the effect holds at every level of every snapshot.}
+{}
+{\cref{tab:coherence}, \cref{tab:coh-years}; \S8.3.}
+
+\QA{What does the pairwise baseline show?}
+{Against native hyperedges, the projection gives the lowest coherence, the lowest perturbation
+robustness (its intervals lie entirely below the static ones at all levels), the most extreme
+giant cluster (5{,}157 nodes at L0) and the most unevaluable labels (114 of 160 at L1).}
+{\item It supports keeping native edge identity and multiplicities.
+\item It is evidence about this projection on this corpus, not a theorem about all pairwise
+methods.}
+{}
+{\S8.1; \cref{tab:headline}, \cref{tab:perturb}, \cref{tab:sizes}.}
+
+\QA{What is the dominant-cluster problem?}
+{In the primary 2026 hierarchy one L0 super-node holds 3{,}765 of 5{,}798 nodes (65\%). It grew
+from 941 nodes in 2020 through 1{,}353 and 2{,}429. All structure-aware variants show it. The
+report's reading: hub nodes connect many hyperedges, merging along them lowers $F$ a lot, and $F$
+has more realised influence than $S$.}
+{\item Largest L0 cluster: semantic 1{,}601, static 3{,}535, pairwise 5{,}157.
+\item A size or balance penalty was deliberately not added after seeing the outputs.
+\item The giant cluster also hurts beam retrieval and dominates ARI.}
+{``The budgets are met'' does not mean ``the levels are balanced or equally useful''.}
+{\S7.8, \S8.1; \cref{tab:sizes}.}
+
+\QA{Are the labels good?}
+{Mixed. The temporal overclaim proxy is $0.318$ at L0 and $0.308$ at L1 ($54/174$ overall).
+Several L0 labels are too generic (``materials'', ``feature'', ``layer''), two clusters share
+``carbon'', one is the fallback, and one (``al'') comes from citation-style names typed
+\code{cited\_work}, which escape the author filter.}
+{\item Static is best at L0 ($0.136$); temporal is best at L1 ($0.308$).
+\item Lexical support for temporal: $0.87$ at L0 and $0.71$ at L1.}
+{A low overclaim rate can reward vagueness.}
+{\S7.5; \cref{tab:labels}, \cref{tab:l0labels}.}
+
+\QA{Which variant would you ship, and why?}
+{The temporal native variant, for a hierarchy that must stay navigable while the corpus grows. It
+has much better continuity, coherence close to static and above chance, perturbation robustness at
+least as good as static, and the best L1 label proxy. The recommendation is about continuity.}
+{\item It does not rest on a demonstrated retrieval benefit or on superior label quality.
+\item The dominant cluster must be addressed before any production use.}
+{}
+{\S8.5.}
+
+\QA{What are the biggest limitations?}
+{Greedy, proposal-dependent optimisation. A dominant cluster. A small corpus (52 papers) with
+annual dates. Generic labels checked only by a general-domain NLI proxy. A heterogeneous,
+incomplete and repeatedly inspected benchmark with 1 of 224 claims aligned. Limited statistical
+power (five seeds, three transitions, no multiple-comparison correction). Work counts that are not
+latency.}
+{\item The results are evidence about this implementation on this corpus.}
+{}
+{\S9.1.}
+
+\QA{What claims can you \emph{not} make?}
+{That $J$ is globally minimised. That 12, 40 and 120 are natural community counts. That temporal
+wins every stability metric. That the coherence effects are large. That the labels are verified as
+faithful. That the hierarchy improves retrieval or latency. That the results transfer to other
+corpora or to larger graphs.}
+{\item Also not claimable: that hyperedges are irreducible to pairs; that material after February
+2026 is excluded; that temporal is more perturbation-robust than static.
+\item Each of these is stated as a limitation or an assumption in the report.}
+{}
+{\cref{tab:verified}; \S9.1; Appendix~\ref{app:emergency}.}
+
+\QA{Why should we trust these results?}
+{Because each number has a control behind it: a different encoder for coherence, type- and
+size-preserving nulls, $t$-intervals over five seeds, held-out label evidence, predictions hashed
+before gold was read, and a 49/49 positive control for the scorer. The merge costs are tested
+against the full objective to $10^{-12}$, and two fresh runs reproduced all scientific outputs.}
+{\item Unflattering results are reported: $0/63$, $\alpha=0.75$ looking better, static beating
+temporal on last-transition ARI.
+\item What that trust does not cover: expert validity of the labels, completeness of the gold
+lists, and generalisation.}
+{Controlled and reproducible is not the same as externally validated.}
+{\S6.1; \cref{tab:verified}.}
+
+\QA{What would you change if you did it again?}
+{The things the report itself diagnoses: the dominant cluster (a size-balance or hub-aware term),
+the retrieval formulation (separate ``find the region'' from ``identify the method node''), and
+the labeller (generic unigrams and citation-like names). Each change would be pre-registered and
+tested on held-out questions, not tuned on this benchmark.}
+{\item Evaluation first: blinded expert audits and a genuinely held-out question set.
+\item The report also flags that $S$ ($\le0.25$) and $F$ ($\le1$) have unequal realised
+influence. Revisiting that scaling is a candidate; the report neither proposes nor tests it.
+\item Whether any of these changes helps: \nest}
+{}
+{\S8.1, \S8.4, \S9.2.}
+
+\QA{What would you do with four more weeks?}
+{In priority order: (1) blinded expert audits of labels, gold targets and claim alignments; (2)
+precise publication dates, for month-level cut-offs; (3) a genuinely held-out question set; (4)
+pre-registered fixes for the diagnosed failures (a balance or hub-aware term, method-typed
+candidate sets inside the selected clusters, an LLM labeller constrained to generation evidence);
+(5) approximate nearest neighbours, only if profiling on a larger corpus requires it.}
+{\item The order follows the task's weighting: evaluation validity before method changes.}
+{}
+{\S9.2.}
+
+\QA{What is your one-minute summary?}
+{``I built a deterministic, hypergraph-native, temporally regularised agglomerative hierarchy with
+hard 12/40/120 display budgets. One objective balances MiniLM semantic dispersion, native
+hyperedge fragmentation and VI to the previous snapshot, and the collapse keeps edge identity,
+arity, multiplicity and provenance. I evaluated it with an independent BGE encoder, type- and
+size-preserving nulls, perturbation intervals, held-out label evidence and frozen retrieval
+predictions. Temporal regularisation raises mean ARI from $0.725$ to $0.892$ for $0.002$ of
+coherence, but one cluster holds 65\% of the nodes, the labels are mixed, and retrieval recovers
+$0/63$ even for the flat baseline.''}
+{\item Next steps: blinded expert audits and a held-out question set, then pre-registered fixes
+for balance and answer-entity retrieval.
+\item In one sentence: a modest, explicit method, honestly evaluated; the continuity result is
+solid and the retrieval result is negative.}
+{}
+{Abstract; \S10; Appendix~\ref{app:emergency}.}
